
# ── Journey 9: Leadership Communication ─────────────────────────────────────
levels.append({'id':81,'journey_id':9,'journey_title':'Leadership Communication','title':'Communicating Vision',
'scenario':'You are the newly appointed head of your department and you have called your team together for the first time. They are a talented group who have been through a period of uncertainty and turnover under previous leadership. You want to communicate a clear, compelling vision that gives them hope, direction, and a reason to invest their best effort.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You open your first address to the team. How do you begin in a way that acknowledges where they have been without dwelling on it?',
'"Before I talk about where I want us to go, I want to acknowledge what you\'ve been through. The last eighteen months have been difficult and I know that. I\'m not here to pretend otherwise — I\'m here to change it."',
['"Exciting times ahead! I\'m so thrilled to be here and I can\'t wait to get started."','Start immediately with your vision without acknowledging the past.','Say, "I\'ve heard there\'s been some challenges — but I think we should focus on the future."'],
'Acknowledging the difficulty with a specific timeframe — eighteen months — before turning to the future signals that you are not dismissing their experience. It earns the right to speak about what comes next.',
'Pure enthusiasm without acknowledgment of the past reads as tone-deaf to a team that has been through difficulty. Starting immediately with the vision suggests the past does not matter to you. "I\'ve heard there\'s been some challenges — but let\'s focus on the future" minimises and pivots without really acknowledging.'),
mcq(2,'You now want to state your vision. Which version is most compelling?',
'"In two years, I want this team to be the first call anyone in this organisation makes when they face our type of challenge. Not because we\'re the most efficient — because we\'re the most thoughtful."',
['"My vision is for us to be a high-performing, aligned team that delivers excellent outcomes."','Say, "I want us to be the best team in this company — I know we can do it."','"We\'re going to focus on execution and continuous improvement this year."'],
'A specific, vivid, behavioural vision — "the first call" — with a specific differentiator — "most thoughtful, not most efficient" — is memorable and gives people something to aim for.',
'"High-performing, aligned team delivering excellent outcomes" is corporate language that sounds like a mission statement and means nothing specific. "The best team — I know we can do it" is enthusiastic but vague. "Focus on execution and continuous improvement" is operational language, not vision.'),
mcq(3,'A team member asks, "What does that mean for our day-to-day work?" How do you make the vision tangible?',
'"Great question. It means when someone brings us a problem, our first move is to understand it fully before we propose a solution. It means we create space for thinking, not just doing. Starting next Monday, here is one specific thing that will change: [specific change]."',
['"It means working smarter, not harder — I\'ll share more detail over the coming weeks."','Say, "Day-to-day, you\'ll know it when you see it — it\'s more of a culture shift."','Promise that everything will become clear once the new strategy document is shared.'],
'Connecting the vision to a specific, observable behaviour — "first move is to understand fully" — and naming one concrete change for next Monday turns an abstract vision into an immediate reality.',
'"Working smarter, not harder" is a cliche that means nothing. "You\'ll know it when you see it" is a culture shift description that defers the tangible. Promising a strategy document to come leaves the team with nothing actionable today.'),
mcq(4,'Another team member asks, "What happens to us if the vision doesn\'t work out?" How do you answer honestly?',
'"That\'s a fair question. I can\'t promise outcomes — I can promise consistent direction, honest communication, and that if we need to change course, you\'ll hear it from me directly and first."',
['Dismiss the concern: "This vision is the right one — I\'m confident it\'ll work."','Promise it will definitely work if everyone commits fully.','Avoid answering directly and say: "Let\'s stay positive and give it our best shot."'],
'Honesty about what you cannot promise — outcomes — paired with what you can promise — direction, communication, transparency about course corrections — is the leadership answer that builds real trust.',
'"This is the right vision — I\'m confident" is a certainty you cannot have. Promising it will definitely work if everyone commits is a false promise that sets up a blame dynamic if it does not. "Let\'s stay positive" is an evasion of an honest question.'),
mcq(5,'You close your address. How do you end in a way that creates forward momentum?',
'"I have one question for each of you before we finish: what is the one thing I should know about this team that I am probably underestimating? I\'m asking because I want to start with you, not with my own assumptions."',
['Close with: "I look forward to working with you all — exciting things ahead!"','Hand out a document summarising your vision for them to review independently.','Ask them to send you their individual goals by end of week so you can align them.'],
'Closing with a question directed at what you might be underestimating signals humility, curiosity, and genuine interest in the team as it actually is — not as you assume it to be.',
'"Exciting things ahead!" is a repeat of the enthusiasm-without-specificity that started the list of wrong options. Handing out a document makes the vision a thing to read rather than something to build together. Asking for individual goals by end of week imposes a process before you have built trust.')],
'response':resp('You acknowledged the difficult past with a specific timeframe, gave a vivid and specific vision with a named differentiator, connected the vision to a specific observable behaviour and one immediate change, were honest about what you could and could not promise, and closed with a question about what you were underestimating. Now type or say aloud the answer you would give if a team member asked: "What does leadership look like to you?"',
'Remarkable! Communicating vision is not about inspiring speeches — it is about giving people a clear picture of a future worth working toward, and making it real enough to step toward today. You did that.')})

levels.append({'id':82,'journey_id':9,'journey_title':'Leadership Communication','title':'Motivating Others',
'scenario':'One of your best team members, Anya, has been quietly disengaged for the past six weeks. She is still delivering her work but the spark is gone — she is not contributing ideas, not staying engaged in team conversations, and seems to be going through the motions. You care about Anya and you want to help her reconnect with her work.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You have asked Anya to join you for coffee. How do you open the conversation?',
'"Anya — I\'ve noticed that over the last few weeks something seems different. You\'re doing the work but you don\'t seem to be as energised as usual. I could be wrong — but I wanted to ask. How are you doing?"',
['Ask Anya directly what is wrong with her performance.','Open with praise: "You\'ve been doing great work — just wanted to check in."','Say, "I\'ve noticed you haven\'t been contributing in meetings — is everything okay?"'],
'Naming a specific observable change — "something seems different, less energised" — without diagnosing the cause, and genuinely asking how she is doing, is the caring and honest opening.',
'"What is wrong with your performance" is inaccurate — her performance is fine — and misframes the concern. Opening with praise without naming the observation you want to address is evasive. "Haven\'t been contributing in meetings" is a specific and potentially slightly accusatory framing of what should be a caring check-in.'),
mcq(2,'Anya pauses and says, "I guess I\'ve been feeling a bit... invisible? Like what I do doesn\'t really matter in the bigger picture." How do you respond?',
'"Thank you for telling me that — it\'s not a small thing to name. What would make the work feel like it matters? I want to understand that specifically."',
['"You\'re not invisible — everyone values your work, I promise."','"I\'m sure that\'s not the case — you do great work and people notice."','Say, "I understand — it can feel that way sometimes. Let\'s push through it."'],
'Thanking her for the specific and vulnerable disclosure, not dismissing it, and asking what would change it — specifically — is genuine and generative.',
'"You\'re not invisible — I promise" is a reassurance that contradicts her experience without asking what generates it. "I\'m sure that\'s not the case" is the same thing in a slightly different frame. "Push through it" is motivational language that treats disengagement as a short-term mood rather than a real signal.'),
mcq(3,'Anya says, "I\'ve been working on the operational stuff for months — but I feel like I never get to work on things that use my real strengths." What is the most useful response?',
'"Tell me more about what you consider your real strengths — and what kinds of work make you feel most alive. I want to see whether we can find a different arrangement that gets more of that into your role."',
['"I know — operational work is important even if it\'s not glamorous."','Say, "We\'ve all got to do operational stuff sometimes — it\'s part of the job."','Promise: "I\'ll give you a better project next quarter."'],
'Asking her to name her strengths and the work that makes her feel most alive is both motivating in itself and practically useful for designing a better role arrangement.',
'"Important even if not glamorous" acknowledges the problem without addressing it. "We all have to do operational stuff" is a comparison that dismisses her specific situation. Promising a "better project next quarter" is vague and deferred.'),
mcq(4,'Anya names two things she is strongest at and most energised by. One of them aligns with an upcoming project you have been planning. How do you use this?',
'"Anya — the project we\'re starting in Q3 is exactly in that space. I\'d like you to lead the element that uses that strength. Can we talk about what that would look like?"',
['Mention the project casually without formalising anything yet.','Say, "I\'ll keep that in mind for when the right opportunity comes up."','Tell Anya she should communicate her interests to HR to be considered for the right projects.'],
'Naming the specific project, naming the specific element she would lead, and immediately proposing a conversation to flesh it out turns a check-in conversation into a motivation intervention with a real outcome.',
'Mentioning it casually leaves the commitment indefinite. "When the right opportunity comes up" defers with no timeline. Directing her to HR removes your personal investment in her motivation, which is exactly what she said she felt the absence of.'),
mcq(5,'Anya smiles for the first time in the conversation and says, "That actually sounds really exciting." How do you close?',
'"I\'m glad. And I want to say — this conversation is the start, not the end. Check in with me in two weeks and tell me how it\'s feeling. And if that spark starts to go again, come earlier rather than later."',
['"Great — I\'m glad we could fix it. I\'ll let you get back to your work."','"I\'m glad you\'re feeling better — you\'re really valuable to this team."','Say, "Perfect — I\'ll let HR know you\'re interested in the Q3 project."'],
'Naming the conversation as the start of a pattern — not a one-off fix — and explicitly inviting her to come earlier next time creates a lasting shift in the relationship.',
'"Glad we could fix it" treats disengagement as a problem with a solution rather than a dynamic that requires ongoing attention. "You\'re really valuable to this team" is an appreciative close but ends on a team-focused note rather than a forward-looking one for Anya specifically. Referring to HR again removes the personal connection.')],
'response':resp('You opened with a caring, specific observation, received her disclosure without dismissing it, asked what would change the feeling specifically, listened to her strengths and found an immediate application, and closed with a sustained engagement rather than a one-time fix. Now type or say aloud the email you would send Anya the next day confirming the Q3 project lead conversation.',
'Outstanding! Motivating others is not about perks or praise — it is about genuinely caring what makes people feel alive in their work and then doing something about it. You did both.')})

levels.append({'id':83,'journey_id':9,'journey_title':'Leadership Communication','title':'Leading Through Change',
'scenario':'Your organisation has just announced a significant restructure that will affect your team. Some roles will change, some may be eliminated, and there is genuine uncertainty. You are meeting with your team to communicate the change. You do not have all the answers but you have a responsibility to communicate honestly, calmly, and in a way that maintains trust.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You open the team meeting to share the news about the restructure. How do you begin?',
'"I want to share some news that will affect us directly and I\'m going to be as straight with you as I can be — including about what I don\'t know yet. I\'d rather give you honest uncertainty than false reassurance."',
['"I have some exciting news about some changes that are coming — it\'s all very positive."','"I\'ve been asked to communicate a restructure announcement to you."','Delay the meeting until you have all the answers to avoid causing unnecessary anxiety.'],
'Framing the conversation as honest, including about uncertainty, and explicitly preferring real uncertainty to false reassurance is the exact tone that maintains trust in change communication.',
'"Exciting news — all very positive" is dishonest framing of a situation that contains genuine risk and uncertainty for team members. "I\'ve been asked to communicate" distances you from the message in a way that signals you do not own it. Delaying until you have all the answers leaves the team in a vacuum where rumour fills the gap.'),
mcq(2,'You share the broad strokes of the restructure. A team member asks, "Are any of our jobs at risk?" How do you answer?',
'"Honestly — yes. I don\'t know which roles will be affected and I won\'t know until the process completes. What I can tell you is that I will tell each of you directly and privately before anything becomes public, and as soon as I know."',
['"I don\'t think anyone here has anything to worry about."','Say, "I can\'t share that information at this stage."','Tell them no roles are at risk to reduce anxiety, even if you are not sure.'],
'Honest acknowledgment that roles are at risk, naming what you do not know, and committing to a specific communication process — direct, private, first — is the courageous and trust-building response.',
'"Nothing to worry about" is a false reassurance that, if wrong, destroys your credibility irreparably. "I can\'t share that information" sounds evasive and escalates anxiety. Telling them no roles are at risk when you are not sure is a lie that may soon unravel.'),
mcq(3,'The team looks anxious. One person says, "How do we keep doing our work when we don\'t know what\'s going to happen to us?" How do you respond?',
'"That\'s exactly the right challenge to name. Here\'s what I\'d ask: let\'s focus on what is in our control — the work in front of us — while I handle what is in mine, which is getting you answers as fast as I can. I won\'t leave you in uncertainty longer than I have to."',
['"Try not to let it affect your work — it\'ll all be resolved soon."','Say, "I know it\'s hard — but we have no choice, so let\'s stay focused."','Promise the team a specific resolution date even if you do not know one.'],
'Dividing responsibility — they focus on the work, you focus on getting answers — and committing not to leave them in uncertainty longer than necessary gives the team a structure for navigating an unstructured moment.',
'"Try not to let it affect your work" is tone-deaf advice in the middle of existential work uncertainty. "We have no choice so let\'s stay focused" is true but motivationally flat. Promising a specific resolution date you do not know creates a promise you cannot keep.'),
mcq(4,'Two weeks later you receive information that one role in the team will be eliminated. Before the formal announcement, how do you handle this?',
'Speak privately to the affected person first, as you promised: "I want to speak with you before anything goes public — the restructure will affect your role directly. I\'m deeply sorry and I want to talk through what support is available."',
['Wait for the formal announcement process to handle the communication.','Tell the whole team at once so no one feels different from anyone else.','Tell the affected person that you "heard something" and they should prepare.'],
'Keeping your personal commitment — private first — is both ethical and trust-maintaining for the whole team, even those not directly affected.',
'Waiting for the formal process when you made a personal commitment to speak first is a breach of your word. Telling the whole team at once treats a deeply personal piece of news as an announcement. Telling them you "heard something" is evasive and creates anxiety without support.'),
mcq(5,'The restructure is finalised and your team is intact except for the one eliminated role. How do you address the team?',
'"We\'ve come through a genuinely hard few weeks. I want to name: one of our colleagues is leaving through no fault of their own, and that matters. For those still here — we have work to do, and I\'m asking you to bring your best. I\'m going to do the same."',
['Lead with positivity: "We made it through! Let me share the exciting new direction."','Say, "I know it\'s sad but let\'s move forward — we have a lot of work to do."','Avoid mentioning the eliminated role so as not to lower morale.'],
'Naming the loss of the colleague as significant before pivoting to forward focus is honest and emotionally intelligent. The team needs to grieve the departure before they can fully commit to what comes next.',
'"We made it through — exciting new direction!" glosses over the real emotional experience of the team with forced positivity. "It\'s sad but let\'s move forward" acknowledges but rushes past the loss. Avoiding any mention of the eliminated role treats it as if it did not happen.')],
'response':resp('You opened with honest uncertainty rather than false reassurance, answered the "are jobs at risk" question truthfully, gave the team a structure for navigating the uncertainty, kept your personal commitment to speak to the affected person first, and honoured the loss before pivoting forward. Now type or say aloud the message you would send the affected colleague a week after they have left, just to check in.',
'Remarkable! Leading through change requires you to be honest about what you do not know, keep every commitment you make, and hold both the pain of transition and the responsibility to move forward at the same time. You did all three.')})

levels.append({'id':84,'journey_id':9,'journey_title':'Leadership Communication','title':'Building Team Culture',
'scenario':'You lead a team of twelve people across two locations. Despite individual talent, the team does not function as a cohesive unit — there is an in-group and out-group between the two locations, communication is transactional, and there is little sense of shared identity or mutual investment. You want to change the culture deliberately.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Before changing anything, you want to understand the current culture. What is the most important first step?',
'Hold individual conversations with each team member in which you ask: "What is working in how we work together, and what is getting in the way?"',
['Send a culture survey to gather data before speaking to anyone.','Meet with the informal leaders in each location and take their view as representative.','Observe the team for a month before doing anything to get a clear picture.'],
'Individual conversations ensure every voice is heard, prevent the dominant voices from shaping the narrative, and signal that you care about each person\'s experience.',
'A survey is useful but removes the conversational quality that builds trust. Taking the informal leaders\' view as representative may reproduce the in-group / out-group dynamic. Observing for a month without signal of intent leaves the team uncertain about your plan.'),
mcq(2,'The conversations reveal that the out-group location feels excluded from key decisions, which are seen as being made by the in-group before meetings and then ratified in meetings. How do you address this?',
'"I want to trial a new decision-making norm: decisions are not finalised until they have been discussed in the meeting. If it is decided before, it should be shared as a proposal, not a done deal."',
['Speak to the in-group location leaders privately and ask them to include the other location more.','Acknowledge the concern but explain that some pre-decisions are necessary for efficiency.','Hold a team session on "communication and inclusion" as a cultural intervention.'],
'A specific, observable norm change — decisions are proposals until the meeting — is the structural intervention that addresses the specific cause of the exclusion.',
'Asking in-group leaders to "include more" without a norm change leaves the structural dynamic in place. Explaining that pre-decisions are necessary validates the behaviour that is creating exclusion. A generic communication session does not address the specific structural issue.'),
mcq(3,'You want to create a ritual that builds cross-location connection. What do you propose?',
'A monthly cross-location "thinking session" of sixty minutes where both locations work on a shared problem — not an operational one — rotating the location that presents the problem.',
['Hold a monthly social event online to help people get to know each other.','Create a shared Slack channel for informal banter across locations.','Schedule bi-weekly all-hands meetings with joint updates from both sides.'],
'A shared thinking session on a real problem — rotating who presents it — creates genuine intellectual community and equal contribution rather than social performance.',
'A social event online can build rapport but does not create shared professional identity. A Slack channel for banter is useful but insufficient for the depth of culture change needed. Bi-weekly all-hands with joint updates is informational, not community-building.'),
mcq(4,'One of the in-group location\'s informal leaders is resistant. He says, "We\'ve always done it this way — it works fine for us." How do you respond?',
'"I understand — it\'s worked for your part of the team. What I\'m trying to build is something that works for all twelve of us. I need your help to do that — not your compliance, your genuine help."',
['Acknowledge his view and make the changes optional for his location.','Tell him the changes are coming regardless and you need his support.','Ask him what he needs in order to get on board with the change.'],
'Naming the distinction between what works for part of the team versus the whole team, and appealing for genuine help rather than compliance, is both honest and motivating.',
'Making changes optional for his location reproduces the two-tier dynamic you are trying to break. "Coming regardless — need your support" is a compliance request dressed up as a consultation. Asking what he needs before he has engaged with the "why" skips the most important part of the conversation.'),
mcq(5,'Six months later, a team member from the previously excluded location says, "It feels different now — I actually feel like part of the same team." How do you close this observation?',
'"That is the best thing I\'ve heard in six months. Thank you for telling me — and for being part of making it different."',
['Say, "I\'m glad — I always knew this team could get there."','Tell the team about this comment at the next all-hands to celebrate the progress.','Say, "That\'s great — keep sharing that kind of feedback, it helps me know what\'s working."'],
'Receiving the observation with specific gratitude — for both telling you and for her participation in the change — closes the moment with genuine warmth and shared ownership of the outcome.',
'"I always knew this team could get there" claims an optimism that others may not have shared and removes the credit from the work everyone did. Sharing it at the all-hands could be powerful, but asking her first before going public is more respectful. "Keep sharing that kind of feedback" turns a powerful moment into a process comment.')],
'response':resp('You grounded the culture work in individual conversations, made a specific norm change to address the structural exclusion, introduced a thinking ritual that builds genuine cross-location community, appealed for genuine help rather than compliance with a resistant informal leader, and received a measure of progress with specific gratitude. Now type or say aloud the one team norm you would propose adding to a new team member\'s onboarding from this point forward.',
'Outstanding! Building team culture is not a single intervention — it is a series of deliberate structural choices that, over time, shape what is normal. You made the right choices and you made them with genuine care. That is how real culture is built.')})

levels.append({'id':85,'journey_id':9,'journey_title':'Leadership Communication','title':'Accountability Without Blame',
'scenario':'A project your team delivered has had a quality issue that has affected a client. The issue stemmed from a combination of factors: tight timeline, a new team member who was under-supervised, and a process that had a gap. You need to address this with the team and with the client in a way that is accountable, honest, and constructive — without scapegoating the new team member or deflecting the accountability that belongs to you.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You meet with your team first. How do you open?',
'"There was a quality issue on the client delivery and I want to understand it fully before we talk about how to prevent it. I\'m not looking for who is to blame — I\'m looking for what in our system failed and what we need to fix."',
['"I need to understand what happened — and why someone let this slip through."','Open by telling the team that the client is very unhappy and that this cannot happen again.','Start by asking the new team member to explain what went wrong.'],
'Framing the inquiry as systemic — what in our system failed — and explicitly naming the non-blame intent is the opening that creates psychological safety for honest retrospection.',
'"Someone let this slip through" is an implicit blame frame. Starting with the client\'s unhappiness makes the team feel attacked. Directing the question at the new team member is scapegoating, even if unintentionally.'),
mcq(2,'The team identifies three factors: the timeline, the supervision gap, and the process gap. You own the supervision gap as a leader. How do you acknowledge this?',
'"The supervision piece is on me. The new team member was in a situation that required more checking-in from me and I did not provide it. That is something I need to do differently."',
['Acknowledge all three as team failures equally.','Focus on the process gap and timeline since those are easier to fix.','Say, "We all share responsibility for this equally."'],
'Specifically and personally owning the supervision failure — naming it clearly as your responsibility — is the leadership act that gives others permission to own their parts without defensiveness.',
'Equal attribution of all three factors blurs the ownership in a way that lets everyone off slightly. Focusing on the easier-to-fix issues avoids the personal ownership. "We all share equally" is a way of distributing the accountability so evenly that no one specifically owns anything.'),
mcq(3,'You are now speaking to the client. How do you open?',
'"I want to speak with you directly about the quality issue on the delivery. I\'m not going to deflect or offer excuses. There were failures in how we managed this project and I take responsibility for them."',
['"We\'ve investigated the issue and it appears to have been caused by some gaps in our process."','"The team worked very hard on this and a few things fell through the cracks despite that."','Open by apologising profusely for five minutes to demonstrate remorse.'],
'Direct, first-person ownership without deflection or excuses is the only appropriate opening with a client who has experienced a quality failure.',
'"Gaps in our process" is a structural framing that distances you from the personal responsibility the client deserves to hear owned directly. "The team worked hard but things fell through the cracks" is a partial deflection onto the team and onto circumstances. Profuse apology without specificity is emotional but not accountable.'),
mcq(4,'The client asks, "What are you going to do to make sure this does not happen again?" How do you answer?',
'"Three specific things: a supervision protocol for all new team members, a timeline review gate before delivery, and a monthly process audit that I own personally. I\'ll send you these in writing by end of week."',
['"We\'re reviewing our processes and will put measures in place."','Promise it will never happen again without explaining how.','Say, "We\'re still working through the response — I\'ll let you know as soon as we have it."'],
'Three specific named measures, with a written commitment and a delivery date, gives the client something tangible to hold you accountable to.',
'"We\'re reviewing our processes" is vague and gives the client no confidence that the response is specific. Promising "never again" without the mechanism is a hollow assurance. "Still working through the response" signals you were not prepared for the most predictable question.'),
mcq(5,'The client says, "I appreciate your honesty — it\'s not what I expected." How do you close?',
'"I take that seriously — because it\'s not how I want our relationship to feel. You should be able to expect this from us as a matter of course, not as a surprise. We\'ll earn that going forward."',
['"Thank you — most people come in with excuses. I don\'t believe in that."','Say, "I\'m glad it came across that way — honesty is one of our company values."','"I appreciate you saying so — let\'s move on to what happens next."'],
'Receiving the observation, naming the aspiration — this should be expected, not surprising — and closing with a forward commitment to earn that standard is genuine and professional.',
'"Most people come in with excuses" makes the observation about other people\'s failings. "Honesty is one of our company values" is corporate and slightly hollow. "Let\'s move on to what happens next" is practical but closes the moment without honouring what the client just offered.')],
'response':resp('You opened the team inquiry as a systemic investigation rather than a blame search, personally owned the supervision failure, addressed the client with direct first-person accountability, gave three specific named measures with a written commitment, and closed with an aspiration — this should be expected, not surprising. Now type or say aloud the written accountability plan you would send the client by end of the week.',
'Remarkable! Accountability without blame is the mark of leadership maturity. You showed that it is possible to own your failures fully while also diagnosing the system — and that doing both, clearly and honestly, is what genuine professional integrity looks like.')})

levels.append({'id':86,'journey_id':9,'journey_title':'Leadership Communication','title':'Inspiring Action',
'scenario':'Your organisation has identified a problem that needs to change but the change requires voluntary effort from people who are already stretched. You have no authority to mandate it. You need to inspire people to act — not by making them feel guilty about not acting, or by using manipulative pressure, but by communicating in a way that makes them want to be part of something.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You are sending a message to colleagues asking them to contribute to a voluntary cross-team initiative. How do you open?',
'"We have identified something that affects all of us and that no single team can fix alone. I want to share what it is and then ask whether you want to be part of fixing it — with no pressure if now is not the right moment for you."',
['"I hope you will all step up for this important initiative — it really needs everyone\'s contribution."','"This is critical and urgent — we need people to act now."','Open with a long explanation of the problem before making any ask.'],
'Naming the shared nature of the problem, stating the ask with explicit permission to decline, is the frame that makes genuine voluntary commitment most likely.',
'"Step up — it needs everyone" uses social pressure rather than genuine invitation. "Critical and urgent — act now" is alarm rather than inspiration. A long explanation before the ask delays the invitation that motivates participation.'),
mcq(2,'You describe the problem. How do you make it feel personally relevant without guilt-tripping?',
'"This problem affects your work directly — specifically [concrete way]. You have probably felt it. I know I have. This initiative is about fixing something that will make all of our work better."',
['Tell people that those who do not participate are part of the problem.','Share a dramatic story about the worst-case scenario if nothing changes.','Say, "This is just the right thing to do" without explaining the personal relevance.'],
'Naming the specific, personal impact of the problem and naming your own experience of it makes it relevant without creating guilt or fear.',
'"Those who do not participate are part of the problem" is guilt-tripping that will generate resentment. A dramatic worst-case scenario is fear-based motivation that typically produces compliance rather than commitment. "It\'s just the right thing to do" is a moral claim that offers no personal relevance.'),
mcq(3,'You make the ask. Someone responds: "I\'d like to help but I\'m already stretched." How do you reply?',
'"Totally valid — and I mean it when I say there\'s no pressure. If your capacity changes, the door is open. In the meantime, is there one person you know who might have room for this?"',
['Express disappointment: "I understand — I just hoped we could count on you."','Try to find a smaller contribution they can make so they are still involved.','Accept the response with no follow-up.'],
'Genuinely accepting the "no" with no pressure, and offering a single low-effort alternative — a referral — is both respectful and practically useful.',
'Expressing disappointment is guilt-tripping dressed up as honesty. Immediately finding a smaller ask when someone says they are stretched tests the boundary they just set. Accepting with absolutely no follow-up leaves the relationship with no forward moment.'),
mcq(4,'Ten people have volunteered. How do you inspire them at the first group meeting?',
'"You are all here because you chose to be. That means something. Let me share exactly what we will do together — and what it will feel like when we\'ve done it."',
['Thank everyone enthusiastically and then immediately assign tasks.','Explain the process in detail so everyone knows what they are getting into.','Share the worst-case scenario again to remind them why this matters.'],
'Honouring their choice, and painting a picture of what success will feel like before assigning any tasks, is the inspirational opening that sets the right tone for voluntary collaboration.',
'Immediately assigning tasks without honouring the contribution of showing up treats it as a work meeting rather than a community effort. A detailed process explanation is useful but not inspiring. Returning to the worst-case scenario to remind them why it matters is fear motivation.'),
mcq(5,'The initiative concludes successfully. How do you close with the ten volunteers?',
'"You changed something. Not because you had to — because you chose to. That is the kind of action that actually moves organisations. I want to name every one of you, with permission, in the summary I send to leadership."',
['Thank them with a group message: "Great work everyone — we did it!"','Buy them a small gift to thank them for their voluntary effort.','Say, "I\'m so grateful — I couldn\'t have done this without you."'],
'Naming what they did — "changed something because they chose to" — framing it as organisationally significant, and asking permission to name them publicly in the leadership summary, is the close that honours and amplifies voluntary action.',
'A group message is warm but does not name the significance of what they chose. A small gift is a nice gesture but does not name the organisational value of what they did. "I couldn\'t have done this without you" makes it about you.')],
'response':resp('You opened with genuine invitation and explicit permission to decline, named the personal relevance without guilt, accepted a "no" genuinely and asked for a referral, honoured the volunteers\' choice before assigning tasks, and closed by naming the significance of voluntary action and amplifying it to leadership with permission. Now type or say aloud the leadership summary you would send to amplify these ten people\'s contribution.',
'Outstanding! Inspiring action — without authority, without pressure, without guilt — is the purest form of leadership communication. It requires you to make the case so compelling, and the invitation so genuine, that people choose to be part of something larger than their immediate workload. You did that.')})

levels.append({'id':87,'journey_id':9,'journey_title':'Leadership Communication','title':'Delivering Hard Truths',
'scenario':'You are meeting with a direct report, Leon, whose performance has been declining for three months. He is technically skilled but his collaboration with the team has deteriorated and he has missed two important deadlines. You have had informal conversations but they have not produced change. Today you need to deliver a harder truth — clearly, specifically, and with genuine care.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You open the meeting. How do you begin?',
'"Leon — I\'m going to be direct with you today because I think you deserve that more than a soft version of what I need to say. Something needs to change in how things are going and I want to talk about it honestly."',
['"I\'ve asked you here to discuss some areas of development."','"I just wanted to check in and see how you\'re feeling about things lately."','Start with extensive praise before transitioning to the hard message.'],
'Naming your intent to be direct, framing it as something Leon deserves, and signalling the seriousness of what follows is the respectful and courageous opening.',
'"Areas of development" is soft language that understates the severity and can allow Leon to avoid the full weight of the message. "How are you feeling about things" is an open check-in when you have a specific and serious message to deliver. Extensive praise before a hard message is the classic "feedback sandwich" that dilutes both parts.'),
mcq(2,'You share the specific issues: two missed deadlines and three specific incidents of poor collaboration. Leon says, "I\'ve been having a tough few months personally." How do you respond?',
'"I hear that — and I\'m genuinely sorry if that\'s been part of this. I want to support you. And I also need to be honest with you: the personal difficulty does not fully explain the collaboration issues from what I\'ve observed. Can we talk about both?"',
['Say, "That changes things — how can I support you through this personal difficulty?"','Dismiss it: "I understand, but personal issues cannot affect professional performance."','Back away from the performance conversation to focus only on the personal support.'],
'Acknowledging the personal difficulty with genuine empathy, while maintaining that the performance conversation still needs to happen, is the most honest and caring response.',
'Pivoting entirely to personal support when a serious performance conversation is needed means the message never lands. "Personal issues cannot affect professional performance" is cold and inaccurate. Backing away from the performance conversation loses the moment and lets the pattern continue.'),
mcq(3,'You have discussed both. Leon says, "I didn\'t realise the collaboration thing was this serious." How do you make the stakes clear?',
'"I want to be very clear: the collaboration with the team has reached a point where it is affecting their ability to do their best work. If things do not change in the next six weeks, I will need to start a formal performance process. I\'m telling you this so you can choose differently."',
['"It\'s serious — but I know you can turn it around."','Say, "I\'m glad you understand — I\'m sure things will improve."','Soften the stakes: "We\'re not at a serious stage yet — this is just a heads up."'],
'Making the stakes explicit — formal process in six weeks if things do not change — with a clear framing that you are telling him so he can choose differently, is honest, specific, and respectful.',
'"I know you can turn it around" is encouraging but avoids the clear statement of consequences. "I\'m sure things will improve" is wishful and removes the urgency. Softening the stakes to "just a heads up" sends the opposite of the message the situation requires.'),
mcq(4,'Leon asks, "What specifically do I need to do differently?" How do you answer?',
'"Two things: first, meet deadlines or communicate a risk to me at least two days before they are at risk — not after. Second, in team interactions — listen before you respond, and when you disagree, name the disagreement rather than going quiet and then working around the decision."',
['Tell Leon to "work on his communication and time management."','Give a long list of everything that has not gone well over the past three months.','Say, "You know what the expectations are — I need you to meet them."'],
'Two specific, observable, actionable behavioural changes are the most useful answer to "what do I need to do differently." The more specific, the more actionable.',
'"Work on communication and time management" is the vaguest possible instruction. Listing everything that has gone wrong shifts from a development conversation to an indictment. "You know what the expectations are" is ambiguous when he has just asked for specificity.'),
mcq(5,'The meeting ends. Leon says, "Thank you for being straight with me." How do you close?',
'"I appreciate that. I didn\'t want to have this conversation and I also knew I had to. I\'m in your corner — I want this to go well for you. Let\'s meet in two weeks to check in."',
['Say, "I hope you heard me — this is serious."','End with encouragement: "I\'m confident you\'ll be fine — don\'t worry."','Close with paperwork: immediately give him a performance note to document the conversation.'],
'Receiving his thanks, naming that you found it hard too, affirming your genuine investment in his success, and committing to a specific follow-up closes the hardest kind of conversation with genuine care.',
'"I hope you heard me — this is serious" re-emphasises the stakes after they have already been acknowledged, which feels like distrust. "Don\'t worry — I\'m confident you\'ll be fine" contradicts the weight of what you just said. Immediately providing paperwork at the close of an honest conversation shifts the tone to a formal process before you have checked whether the message landed.')],
'response':resp('You opened with directness framed as respect, received a personal difficulty with empathy while maintaining the performance conversation, made the stakes completely clear, gave two specific and observable behavioural changes, and closed with genuine care and a specific follow-up commitment. Now type or say aloud the notes you would make in Leon\'s file immediately after this meeting.',
'Remarkable! Delivering hard truths is one of the hardest things a leader ever has to do. You showed that it is possible to be clear about the seriousness of a situation, specific about what needs to change, and genuinely caring about the person at the same time. That is leadership.')})

levels.append({'id':88,'journey_id':9,'journey_title':'Leadership Communication','title':'Inclusive Communication',
'scenario':'You lead a diverse team and you are aware that your communication has sometimes, without intention, favoured the louder, more confident voices. You have also received feedback that the team members for whom English is not a first language sometimes feel left behind in fast-moving meetings. You want to become a more deliberately inclusive communicator.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You are preparing for your next team meeting. What changes do you make to the meeting design?',
'Share the agenda with specific discussion questions 24 hours in advance, and add a "contribution pause" at each agenda item — thirty seconds before opening discussion — for everyone to form their thoughts.',
['Make the meeting shorter so it is less demanding for non-native speakers.','Ask the most confident speakers to hold back so quieter voices get more space.','Move all discussions to written channels where everyone can contribute at their own pace.'],
'Sharing the agenda and questions in advance gives everyone — especially those who process best before the moment — time to prepare. The "contribution pause" prevents the fastest speakers from dominating.',
'Shorter meetings reduce the problem but do not address the dynamic. Asking confident speakers to hold back is a management of the symptom, not the cause. Moving to written channels removes the value of real-time discussion.'),
mcq(2,'In the meeting, you notice a team member, Min, trying to enter the conversation but being repeatedly talked over. How do you intervene?',
'"Let me pause us here — Min, I saw you start to speak. Please go ahead."',
['Say, "Min — do you have something to add?" in a tone that singles her out.','Continue and speak to Min privately after the meeting.','Ask the group to slow down and let people finish their thoughts.'],
'A direct, specific, respectful intervention that names Min and returns the floor to her is the minimum effective action. It does not shame anyone and it is immediate.',
'"Min — do you have something to add?" is slightly condescending in tone and questions her contribution before she has made it. Speaking privately after the meeting means the exclusion stood in the moment. A general "let people finish" addresses everyone and no one.'),
mcq(3,'After a complex discussion, you want to check that everyone has understood the decision. How do you do this inclusively?',
'Write the decision on the shared screen or whiteboard and say: "Here is what I heard us agree. Does this capture it accurately?" and give thirty seconds before asking for responses.',
['Ask, "Does everyone understand?" and wait for raised hands.','Assume understanding since no one asked clarifying questions.','Ask the strongest communicator to summarise the discussion for the group.'],
'Writing the decision and asking whether it is accurate — rather than asking who understood — removes the social cost of admitting confusion and gives everyone time to check comprehension.',
'"Does everyone understand?" with raised hands is a social check, not a comprehension check. Assuming understanding from absence of questions assumes the most confident people\'s silence represents everyone\'s. Asking the strongest communicator to summarise centres their voice rather than your own neutral summary.'),
mcq(4,'You are giving feedback to a team member whose spoken English is strong but written communication is harder for others to parse. How do you raise this?',
'"I want to share some feedback on your written communications — they sometimes have a different tone or structure to what the team expects, and I think we can make a simple adjustment. Can I show you what I mean with one example?"',
['Ask another team member to proofread everything he writes before it is sent.','Avoid raising it to prevent causing embarrassment.','Say, "Your writing is a bit unclear sometimes — you should work on it."'],
'A specific, constructive framing — different tone or structure than expected, here is an example — is respectful and actionable without being condescending.',
'Asking another team member to proofread is well-intentioned but infantilises him and creates a dependency. Avoiding the feedback leaves the problem in place. "A bit unclear — you should work on it" is vague and blunt.'),
mcq(5,'After three months of these deliberate changes, a team member says, "This team feels different — I feel more able to speak up." How do you respond?',
'"I\'m really glad to hear that. These were deliberate choices and I\'ll keep making them. Is there anything still getting in the way for you?"',
['Say, "Great — the agenda change was the main thing."','Celebrate with the whole team to make the change visible to everyone.','Just say, "That\'s great to hear!" without asking for more information.'],
'Acknowledging that the changes were deliberate — which reinforces that they will continue — and immediately asking what else might still be a barrier shows that inclusion is ongoing, not a one-time intervention.',
'Naming the agenda change as the main thing is reductive when multiple changes were made. Celebrating with the whole team publicly is a good idea but secondary to the one-to-one response. "That\'s great to hear" is warm but misses the opportunity to deepen the feedback.')],
'response':resp('You redesigned the meeting structure to support preparation and reflection, intervened directly when someone was talked over, wrote the decision to make comprehension inclusive, gave respectful written communication feedback with an example, and kept the door open for more feedback after three months. Now type or say aloud one more change you would make to your communication habits in the next month based on what you have learned.',
'Outstanding! Inclusive communication is not a checklist — it is a practice of noticing who is not fully present in a conversation and making a specific, consistent choice to change that. You showed what that practice looks like in real leadership.')})

levels.append({'id':89,'journey_id':9,'journey_title':'Leadership Communication','title':'Coaching Conversations',
'scenario':'You have a regular one-on-one with your direct report, Jade, who has been in her role for eighteen months. She is capable and ambitious and she wants to grow — but she tends to bring you her problems to solve rather than developing her own problem-solving capacity. You want to shift this dynamic by using a more coaching-oriented approach.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Jade opens the one-on-one with: "I\'ve been stuck on the client proposal — I\'m not sure how to approach the pricing section. What do you think I should do?" How do you respond?',
'"That\'s a good challenge. Before I share my view — what options have you already considered? I want to hear your thinking first."',
['"Here\'s what I would do..."','Say, "You know how to do this — you\'ve done it before."','Tell her to draft something and bring it back next week.'],
'Returning the question to Jade before giving your view — by asking what she has already considered — is the coaching move that builds her problem-solving capacity rather than substituting yours.',
'"Here\'s what I would do" solves the immediate problem and reinforces the dynamic you are trying to change. "You know how to do this" is encouraging but does not develop the thinking. "Draft something and bring it back" defers without building anything.'),
mcq(2,'Jade says, "I thought about two approaches — standard cost-plus or a value-based model, but I can\'t decide." How do you move her forward?',
'"What are the factors that would make one better than the other in this specific case? What do you know about this client\'s priorities?"',
['Tell her which approach you would choose and why.','Ask her which one she feels more comfortable with.','Say, "It depends on many factors — let me think about it."'],
'Asking her to apply the decision-making framework — what factors, what do you know about this client — builds analytical capability rather than comfort-based choice.',
'Telling her your choice solves the immediate problem but does not develop hers. "Which one are you more comfortable with" is a comfort-based rather than analysis-based question. "Let me think about it" takes back the problem you are trying to return to her.'),
mcq(3,'Jade identifies that the client has previously prioritised total cost over premium options. She concludes: "I think cost-plus makes more sense for this client." How do you respond?',
'"I think you\'re right — and you got there with your own analysis. What made it click for you?"',
['Immediately confirm she is right without asking her to reflect.','Add your own additional reasoning to validate her conclusion.','Move immediately to the next topic without anchoring the insight.'],
'Confirming she is right and immediately asking what made it click consolidates the insight and builds her confidence in her own analytical process — not just the conclusion.',
'Immediately confirming without the reflective question misses the opportunity to anchor the process. Adding your own reasoning makes it a joint conclusion rather than hers. Moving on immediately loses the learning moment.'),
mcq(4,'Later in the one-on-one, Jade brings another problem to you: "The project timelines are slipping — what should I do?" How do you coach rather than direct?',
'"What do you think are the top two causes of the slippage? And what options do you see for addressing each?"',
['Give her a project recovery plan from your own experience.','Say, "I\'ll get involved in the project directly to help stabilise it."','Tell her to put together a risk log and report back.'],
'Asking for root causes and options again is the coaching pattern that, over time, becomes her default analytical approach.',
'A recovery plan from your own experience solves the problem but removes her from the driver\'s seat. Getting involved directly in the project removes her ownership. A risk log is a useful tool but asking her to compile it and report back does not build the problem-solving thinking.'),
mcq(5,'At the end of the one-on-one, Jade says, "I feel like I\'m actually thinking through things more clearly — is that the point of these conversations?" How do you close?',
'"That is exactly the point. My job in these conversations is not to give you answers — it\'s to help you find your own. The goal is for you to eventually not need me for these conversations at all."',
['Say, "Yes — I\'ve been coaching you. You should look into some coaching frameworks yourself."','Say, "I\'m glad you noticed — it\'s an approach I read about recently."','Just say, "Yes!" without explaining the intent.'],
'Naming the goal explicitly — you will not need me for this — is both honest and genuinely empowering. It names the destination of the coaching relationship.',
'"You\'ve been coaching you — look into frameworks" is slightly patronising and self-referential. "An approach I read about recently" is an oddly humble deflection. Just "yes!" without the explanation leaves the insight uncompleted.')],
'response':resp('You asked what Jade had already considered before giving your view, asked her to apply analytical factors rather than comfort-based choice, consolidated the insight by asking what made it click, asked for causes and options when the second problem arrived, and named the goal of the coaching relationship explicitly. Now type or say aloud the three questions you would use most often to turn a problem-bringing one-on-one into a coaching conversation.',
'Brilliant! A coaching approach to leadership is one of the most valuable gifts you can give a direct report. You are not solving their problems — you are helping them become the person who solves their own. That is genuine professional development.')})

levels.append({'id':90,'journey_id':9,'journey_title':'Leadership Communication','title':'Strategic Communication',
'scenario':'Your organisation is about to make a significant strategic pivot. As a senior leader, you need to communicate this pivot to three different audiences: your team, your external partners, and the public. Each audience needs different framing, different levels of detail, and a different tone. You want to prepare for all three.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Before communicating the pivot at all, what is the most important preparation step?',
'Define the one-sentence version of the pivot that is true, clear, and can anchor every communication that follows.',
['Prepare the most detailed version first and then abbreviate for different audiences.','Survey each audience about what they want to hear before preparing anything.','Wait for the CEO to communicate first so you can align with the company narrative.'],
'A single true anchoring sentence ensures that every audience-specific version is a coherent variation of the same story, not a different story for each audience.',
'Starting with the most detailed version risks building complexity before you have clarity. Surveying audiences before preparing positions you as reactive rather than leading. Waiting for the CEO removes your ownership of communicating to your stakeholders.'),
mcq(2,'Your team communication. The pivot means some of their current work will be deprioritised. How do you frame this for your team?',
'"Our strategy is changing in a way that will affect some of the work we\'re currently doing. I want to be honest with you about what that means, help you understand the thinking behind it, and talk about how we\'re going to navigate this together."',
['Tell the team the pivot is exciting news and a huge opportunity.','Focus only on the new strategic direction without naming what is being deprioritised.','Wait until the deprioritisation becomes visible in their workloads before saying anything.'],
'Honest, specific framing that names the impact on current work, explains the thinking, and offers to navigate it together is respectful and trust-maintaining.',
'"Exciting news and a huge opportunity" is dishonest when work they care about is being deprioritised. Focusing only on the new direction without naming what is being deprioritised lets people discover the impact without the courtesy of advance communication. Waiting until it becomes visible means the team learns it by accident.'),
mcq(3,'Your external partner communication. Partners need to know how the pivot affects their relationship with you. How do you approach this?',
'Contact key partners proactively before any public announcement: "I wanted to speak with you before this becomes public. Our strategy is shifting in [direction] and I want to talk through what that means for our partnership."',
['Wait for partners to ask and then respond.','Send a blanket email to all partners simultaneously.','Let the public announcement speak for itself.'],
'Proactive, personal contact before the public announcement signals that the partnership matters and gives you control of the narrative in that relationship.',
'Waiting for partners to ask means they may hear the news from another source first. A blanket email to all partners is impersonal and does not differentiate important relationships. Letting the announcement speak for itself removes the relationship from the communication.'),
mcq(4,'Your public communication. The pivot involves exiting a product line that some customers use. How do you handle this?',
'"We are making a strategic change that affects [product line]. We\'re committed to supporting all current users through the transition and want to be transparent about the timeline. Here is what you need to know: [specific, clear timeline and next steps]."',
['Frame it as the product "evolving" rather than being exited.','Announce the exit without offering any transition support information.','Delay the public announcement to reduce negative reaction.'],
'Clear, honest language about the exit, combined with a specific commitment to transition support and a clear timeline, is the only appropriate public communication.',
'"Evolving" is euphemistic language that customers will quickly see through when they realise the product is being removed. Announcing without transition support information leaves customers without what they most need. Delaying the announcement allows speculation and rumour to fill the gap.'),
mcq(5,'A journalist asks: "Was this strategic pivot a sign that the previous strategy failed?" How do you respond?',
'"The previous strategy served a purpose in its time and taught us things we could not have learned any other way. The new direction is built on that learning. We call that progress, not failure."',
['Say, "No comment" on anything strategy-related.','Admit that the previous strategy did fail in order to seem honest.','Deflect: "I\'m here to talk about the new direction, not the past."'],
'Acknowledging the value of the previous strategy — it served a purpose and taught us things — without conceding failure, and framing the pivot as a product of learning, is the honest and professionally effective response.',
'"No comment" signals evasion and invites further probing. "Yes, it failed" is technically possible but frames the story negatively without nuance. "I\'m here to talk about the new direction" is a deflection that a journalist will immediately notice and probe.')],
'response':resp('You defined an anchoring one-sentence version of the pivot, communicated honestly with your team about the impact on current work, contacted partners proactively before the public announcement, gave clear and specific public communication about the product exit with transition support, and reframed the journalist\'s question about failure with honest nuance. Now type or say aloud the one-sentence anchor statement you would use for this strategic pivot across all three audiences.',
'Outstanding! Strategic communication is the discipline of telling the same true story in three different languages — for three different audiences with three different needs — without contradicting yourself or losing the thread. You did that. That is leadership communication at its highest level.')})
